  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "If $f(x,y)=xy+x{e}^{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $\u0394f$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y+{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}x+(x+x{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,0) & =(0+{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}x+(2+2\\cdot {e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+4\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+\u0394x,0+\u0394y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\u0394f\u22481\\,\u0394x+4\\,\u0394y.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394x & =1-2=-1, \\\\ \u0394y & =-0.5-0=-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394f & \u22481\\,\u0394x+4\\,\u0394y \\\\  & \u22481\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & \u2248-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $3.$",
